---
import {
  repeatedExecutionAblation,
  screeningAblation,
} from "../data/ablations";
import PublicationTable from "./PublicationTable.astro";
import ResultValue from "./ResultValue.astro";
const figures = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}figures/`;

const repeatedQualityGain = (
  repeatedExecutionAblation[3].values[0] -
  repeatedExecutionAblation[1].values[0]
).toFixed(2);
const screeningQualityGain = (
  screeningAblation[1].bayesian[0] - screeningAblation[1].meanBased[0]
).toFixed(2);
---

<div class="ablation-studies">
  <article
    class="ablation-study"
    id="rq1"
    aria-labelledby="gate-ablation-heading"
  >
    <h3 id="gate-ablation-heading" class="publication-heading">
      RQ1: How does Pareto-aware promotion enable better optimization outcomes?
    </h3>
    <p>
      <strong
        >Pareto-aware promotion preserves useful intermediate trade-offs.</strong
      >
      In the controlled PS-MinCost comparison, it promotes 11 candidates rather than
      3 and reaches a final harness with higher test quality and lower cost. Only
      the local promotion gate is changed.
    </p>
    <div class="ablation-comparison">
      <figure class="ablation-figure">
        <img
          src={`${figures}ablation-local-gates.svg`}
          width="1044"
          height="840"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          alt="ClawMark development quality–cost trajectories under Pareto and MinCost local gates. The Pareto trajectory continues through I-11 to later quality gains; an inset compares the final held-out quality and cost."
        />
        <figcaption>
          Pareto and MinCost local gates lead to different optimization paths.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="ablation-analysis">
      <p>
        The search paths diverge around <strong>I-11</strong>. Its mini-batch
        quality rises from 83.65% to 87.62%, while cost increases by 10.5%. A
        MinCost gate would reject this update. Pareto promotion keeps it
        available for broader evaluation and later optimization toward I-14 and
        I-16.
      </p>
      <p>
        A quality-only gate can also discard useful candidates. In the PS-Pareto
        run, I-7 lowers mini-batch cost by 17.22% with a 1.33-point quality
        decrease. Development evaluation instead shows higher quality (72.94%
        vs. 72.27%) and lower cost ($0.88 vs. $1.13). These cases illustrate why <strong
          >intermediate promotion and final deployment selection should remain
          separate</strong
        >: a locally unfavorable trade-off can still support a better final
        harness.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <article
    class="ablation-study"
    id="rq2"
    aria-labelledby="execution-ablation-heading"
  >
    <h3 id="execution-ablation-heading" class="publication-heading">
      RQ2: How do repeated executions improve harness optimization?
    </h3>
    <p>
      <strong
        >Repeated execution improves the evidence used to update a harness.</strong
      >
      Moving from one to three executions per task during optimization raises mean
      test quality by {repeatedQualityGain} points at the quality-oriented endpoint.
      The cost-oriented endpoint also reaches higher quality at lower cost.
    </p>
    <div class="ablation-comparison">
      <PublicationTable
        id="execution-ablation-table"
        label="ClawMark single-run versus three-run PS-Pareto ablation"
      >
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Setting</th>
            <th scope="col">Quality (%) ↑</th>
            <th scope="col">Cost per task ($) ↓</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        {
          repeatedExecutionAblation.map((row) => (
            <tbody class:list={{ "is-ours": row.preferred }}>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row" class="method-label">
                  {row.label}
                  <sup>{row.endpoint}</sup>
                </th>
                <td>
                  <ResultValue
                    mean={row.values[0]}
                    deviation={row.values[1]}
                    emphasis={
                      row.preferred && row.endpoint === "Q" ? "best" : undefined
                    }
                  />
                </td>
                <td>
                  <ResultValue
                    mean={row.values[2]}
                    deviation={row.values[3]}
                    emphasis={
                      row.preferred && row.endpoint === "C" ? "best" : undefined
                    }
                  />
                </td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          ))
        }
      </PublicationTable>
      <figure class="ablation-figure">
        <img
          src={`${figures}ablation-repeated-executions.svg`}
          width="972"
          height="948"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          alt="Quality versus cost for six executions of each of four sampled ClawMark tasks. Marker colors distinguish tasks, and the legend reports within-task coefficients of variation for quality and cost."
        />
        <figcaption>
          Six executions per task reveal variation in both quality and cost.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="ablation-analysis">
      <p>
        Repeated runs expose <strong
          >persistent failures, mixed outcomes, and efficiency contrasts</strong
        >. They reveal recurring defects, unstable behavior on the same task,
        and unnecessary execution cost even when every run succeeds. Comparing
        the parent and candidate repeatedly also helps verify whether a patch's
        improvement persists.
      </p>
      <p>
        For example, three successful research-audit executions cost $0.72,
        $1.58, and $0.69. The expensive trajectory makes three subagent-spawn
        calls; the others make none. A single passing trajectory would miss this
        contrast. Repeated observations give the optimizer concrete alternatives
        to investigate when reducing execution overhead.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <article
    class="ablation-study"
    id="rq3"
    aria-labelledby="screening-ablation-heading"
  >
    <h3 id="screening-ablation-heading" class="publication-heading">
      RQ3: Does Bayesian screening improve promotion under execution
      uncertainty?
    </h3>
    <p>
      <strong
        >Bayesian screening reaches higher mean quality at lower cost at both
        endpoints.</strong
      >
      At the quality-oriented endpoint, it gains {screeningQualityGain} test quality
      points while reducing cost from $1.15 to $1.01 per task. The remaining optimization
      setup is held fixed.
    </p>
    <div class="ablation-comparison">
      <PublicationTable
        id="screening-ablation-table"
        label="ClawMark mean-based versus Bayesian screening under PS-Pareto"
      >
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Setting</th>
            <th scope="col" class="metric-heading">Metric</th>
            <th scope="col">Mean-based</th>
            <th scope="col" class="preferred-cell">Bayesian</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        {
          screeningAblation.map((row) => (
            <tbody>
              {([0, 2] as const).map((metric) => (
                <tr>
                  {metric === 0 && (
                    <th rowspan="2" scope="rowgroup" class="method-label">
                      PS-Pareto<sup>{row.endpoint}</sup>
                    </th>
                  )}
                  <th scope="row" class="metric-label">
                    {metric === 0 ? "Qual. ↑" : "Cost ↓"}
                  </th>
                  <td>
                    <ResultValue
                      mean={row.meanBased[metric]}
                      deviation={row.meanBased[metric + 1]}
                    />
                  </td>
                  <td class="preferred-cell">
                    <ResultValue
                      mean={row.bayesian[metric]}
                      deviation={row.bayesian[metric + 1]}
                      emphasis="best"
                    />
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          ))
        }
      </PublicationTable>
      <figure class="ablation-figure">
        <img
          src={`${figures}ablation-promotion-threshold.svg`}
          width="1152"
          height="840"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          alt="Number of archived proposals that would be promoted on ClawMark and GAIA2 as the promotion threshold increases from 0.5 to 0.95. A shaded band marks the experimental setting of 0.7."
        />
        <figcaption>
          Retrospective threshold replay; the shaded region marks γ = 0.7,
          within a locally stable region on both benchmarks.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="ablation-analysis">
      <p>
        <strong>Averages can conceal uneven task-level changes.</strong>
        Replaying mean-based screening on the same 17 PS-Pareto candidates would promote
        14, compared with 12 under Bayesian screening. I-12 reduces mean mini-batch
        cost by 7.19% with a 5.55-point quality loss, satisfying the configured mean-based
        rule. Its bootstrap support is only 0.55, below the 0.7 promotion threshold.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nearly all of I-12's savings come from one research-assistant task where
        quality drops sharply; the other two tasks become more expensive. On the
        development set, I-12 is 2.80 quality points worse and 10.66% more
        expensive than its parent. This illustrates the value of
        <strong
          >screening for statistical support across repeated executions</strong
        >. The separate end-to-end comparison above measures how the screening
        rule affects subsequent search and the final selected harnesses.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</div>
